; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The destruction receipt: what a deployment signs when it has taken material out of the copies it holds,
; and can say which copies a destruction reached and which it did not.
;
; A pack attests that the responses of one window are all of them. A redaction attests that a named record
; came out of a closed pack. Neither says that anything is gone: a redaction states an excision from one
; document and is silent about every other place those bytes sit. Under an erasure instruction the question
; a controller has to answer is the second one, and it has an answer only per copy: the deployment's own
; store, the sealed packs and exports it keeps, the media it backs up onto, and the copies that left its
; hands. The first four are inside a deployment's reach and the fifth is not, and a document that blurred
; that line would be the one artifact of this estate that overstates itself.
;
; So this container states a reach per copy class, and the format closes the list: every class it enumerates
; carries exactly one statement, and the class it declares out of reach may only ever be stated
; `not-reached`. An artifact that quietly claimed a complete erasure would be worse than refusing, which is
; the same reason the redaction refuses to state that a pack's head still holds.
;
; `packages/receipt/src/destruction-receipt.ts` reads this layout and writes it, and refuses each shape it
; will not accept by code. `packages/receipt/schemas/destruction-receipt-v1.schema.json` is its
; machine-readable twin, and `packages/receipt/test/destruction-receipt.test.ts` holds the members this file
; declares, that twin, the reader's own list and the shipped error-code union against each other, so no one
; of them can widen alone.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ashaveri-Destruction-Receipt = #6.18(COSE_Sign1-Destruction-Receipt-COSE)

; The envelope every signed document in this estate uses, unchanged but for the content type: COSE_Sign1 per
; RFC 9052 section 4.2, encoded per RFC 8949 with Core Deterministic Encoding, so a reader rebuilds the bytes
; the issuer signed rather than guessing which of several equivalent spellings was used.
COSE_Sign1-Destruction-Receipt-COSE = [
    protected: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Destruction-Receipt-Protected-Header,
    unprotected: { * any => any }, ; the map that carries no claim; see the note below the manifest
    payload: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Destruction-Receipt-Manifest,
    signature: bstr .size 64
]

; The same three labels a receipt, a pack, an export, a redaction and an epoch inventory sign with, and a
; sixth content type. One deployment key seals all six, each passes its own checks, and a reader that took a
; destruction receipt for a redaction would report an excision from a pack where the bytes state a reach over
; five copy classes. That mistake is not recoverable afterwards, because both documents verify.
Ashaveri-Destruction-Receipt-Protected-Header = {
    1: -8,                                 ; alg: EdDSA (Ed25519)
    4: bstr .size 32,                      ; kid: sha256(public_key)
    3: "ashaveri/destruction-receipt",     ; typ
}

; The signed manifest. `v: 1` is one version, and it says what the fields attest rather than which software
; wrote them, as it does in `receipt.cddl`, `pack.cddl`, `export.cddl` and `redaction.cddl`. Nothing has ever
; emitted a destruction receipt, so there is no document in an older shape for a second number to be a rule
; about, and a reader of `v: 1` refuses any other number before it reads a member.
Ashaveri-Destruction-Receipt-Manifest = {
    v: 1,                        ; format version
    at: int,                     ; unix seconds, the instant the destruction was stated
    object: bstr .size 32,       ; sha256 of the exact bytes this destruction took
    kind: tstr,                  ; which document those bytes are, from the registry below
    key: bstr .size 32,          ; the kid of the destroyed key: sha256 of its public half
    reach: [ + reach-entry ],    ; exactly one entry per copy class, at least one entry
    states: tstr .size (1..2048) ; the writer's own sentence stating this destruction
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `object` and `kind`, the designation. `object` is sha256 over the exact bytes the destruction took, and
; `kind` names which document those bytes are. Both are required and neither is decorative.
;
; A digest alone names no document. The estate's own ruling on a digest is that it names the document it
; digests, and a reader handed `object` with no `kind` could not tell a destroyed receipt from a destroyed
; pack, which are two different findings about two different amounts of material. A `kind` alone is a label
; nothing checks. Together they are one claim a reviewer can test twice: hash what you hold, compare the
; digest, then open the bytes and read the content type their own signed header carries and compare that.
;
; Three readings of the object were available and the other two are worse. An id the writer chose is a
; string a reader cannot check against anything, which is the fault `redaction.cddl` refuses beside its own
; designation. The bytes themselves are not carryable: they are what was destroyed, and an artifact
; proving an erasure by re-publishing the erased material is a contradiction. A digest of a canonical
; re-encoding of the object would need the reviewer to hold a tool that can produce that re-encoding, while
; a digest of the bytes as handed over needs only the bytes.
;
; The registry is the set of documents this estate signs and can therefore name without inventing a
; vocabulary for them. A class outside it is refused rather than read as an unnamed thing: widening the
; registry is a decision about the format and not a value a writer picks.
;   "ashaveri/receipt", "ashaveri/pack", "ashaveri/export",
;   "ashaveri/redaction", "ashaveri/epoch-inventory"
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `key`, the destroyed key's identifier, and the whole of what this layout can say about a key.
;
; Key material is secret material and is never a repository artifact, so these bytes name a key and carry
; none of it. The name is the kid: sha256 over the key's public half, spelled exactly as label 4 of the
; protected header spells one, which is a public label and not a secret. Two 32-byte positions exist in this
; manifest, `object` and `key`, and the map is closed, so a writer has no position to put a seed, a keypair,
; a wrapped key or an exported PEM in: adding one is a version change that a reader of `v: 1` refuses, and a
; value of any other width at either position is refused as a malformed manifest. A 64-byte Ed25519 public
; key and a 32-byte secret seed both fail on width at `key`, which is the check doing the work rather than a
; rule about names.
;
; What the reader can settle is that the statement was not made by the key it names. `key` equal to the kid
; in the header is refused as `DESTRUCTION_RECEIPT_KEY_IS_SIGNER`: a document sealed by a key is proof that
; the holder of that key signed it at or after the instant the document stamps, so a receipt that destroyed
; its own signing key would be signing its own refutation. Attestation therefore arrives from a key that is
; still in hand, and the destroyed key is spoken of from outside.
;
; What no reader can settle from these bytes is stated rather than skipped: that the named key cannot sign
; again. Nothing here measures that. A reviewer who wants it asks a question outside this document, whether
; the kid produces a valid signature over anything stamped after `at`, and this format neither claims the
; answer nor supplies it.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `reach`, the copy classes, and the reason the list is closed rather than chosen.
;
; One entry per class, every class present, no class named twice. The classes are where a copy of a
; deployment's material can sit:
;   "store"            the deployment's own receipt store
;   "pack"             sealed evidence packs the deployment holds
;   "export"           export containers the deployment holds
;   "backup"           backup media the deployment holds
;   "handout"          a copy that left the deployment's hands, given to a customer or another party
;
; A missing class is refused as its own code and not as a malformed document, because it is not a typo: a
; receipt that says nothing about `backup` reads, to anyone holding it, like a receipt that reached
; everything except what it named. Silence is the fault this list exists to make unproducible, and it is the
; same fault `receipt.cddl` refuses with the four members it adds so that a mark, a stamp's source, an
; appraisal's context and an item list are attested rather than passed over.
;
; `handout` is out of reach and the format says so structurally. Once bytes are given to another party, no
; act this deployment takes reaches them, so `outcome: 1` at that class is refused as
; `DESTRUCTION_RECEIPT_REACH_OVERREACH` by the reader and cannot be produced by the writer. Stating
; `not-reached` there is not a defect of the receipt; it is the honest half of what it states, and it is the
; only statement about a third party's copies this container admits. A deployment that genuinely holds a
; contractual route to a customer's copy still states `not-reached` here, because what this field reports is
; what this destruction reached, and a route is not a reach.
;
; `outcome` is 1 for `reached` and 2 for `not-reached`. Two labels spelled as text were the alternative, and
; the numbers are chosen because a boolean was the third: a boolean reads as "reached or unknown" and invites
; a writer to leave the hard class off, which is the silence the completeness rule refuses; two integers
; named in one place have no default and no falsy value to fall into.
reach-entry = {
    class: tstr,     ; one label from the closed registry above
    outcome: 1 / 2   ; 1 reached, 2 not-reached
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `at`, the only clock here, and the one relation it is bounded by inside these bytes.
;
; `at` is the instant the destruction was stated, and it is never before the epoch, as in every other
; container of this estate. Nothing in this document stamps the object, because the object is gone and the
; only other signed instant a reader might have weighed it against belongs to a document this receipt does
; not require: a destruction receipt is handed over by itself far more often than a redaction is, and one
; that could not be read without its pack would state nothing at all on its own. So there is no cross-document
; ordering to check, and `at` carries no bound it cannot be checked against alone.
;
; Freshness is not this container's question either. A receipt stamped in 2026 states a destruction stated in
; 2026 and says nothing about whether the copies still match today; a reader that cares about that owns the
; question and says so out loud, as `docs/receipt-spec.md` states beside the verification algorithm.
;
; `states` is the writer's own sentence, bounded so it cannot be empty and free as to what it says, inside the
; signature and outside every recomputation. It carries no control character and no character of the
; printed-line class, because a sentence that can end or reorder the row it is printed on is not a sentence
; about a destruction but a way of forging one. No reason vocabulary is defined here: no article, no duty, no
; ground and no period appears as a member, a label or an enumeration in this file, for the reason
; `redaction.cddl` gives, that a format enumerating answers would itself be an answer.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Closure is one rule across this file and not a rule about one map, as `receipt.cddl`, `pack.cddl`,
; `export.cddl` and `redaction.cddl` state it. No block above carries a `...`, and the absence means here what
; it means there: a member the version named by `v` does not define makes the document invalid rather than a
; document read with the unexpected member dropped. That reaches the manifest, each entry of `reach` and the
; signed header alike. The header closes under the stronger reason: its bytes are hashed into the
; `Sig_structure`, so a label this block does not name is an authenticated parameter, and a reader that took
; its three labels out of a header carrying a fourth would hand on a document other than the one the issuer
; signed.
;
; One map a signer fills at will, and that is a decision rather than a gap: the `unprotected` map of
; `COSE_Sign1-Destruction-Receipt-COSE` sits outside the signature, so nothing written in it can travel as a
; claim about a destruction, and enforcing that it is empty would buy strictness with no content behind it.
;
; Every number above written `int` is a CBOR integer, major type 0 or 1, and no other major type carries one,
; including `outcome`, which is the one number a reader is most likely to compare loosely. A floating-point
; number whose value happens to be a whole number is a different type, and only a reader reading the bytes can
; tell them apart, because a decoder that hands both over as one number leaves a check placed afterwards
; nothing to distinguish. So a reader decodes every map this file names member by member, in a value and in a
; key alike, where no floating-point number may appear. Sign is that rule's other half: every instant this
; file names is a unix time no earlier than the epoch, so a negative stamp is a malformed document rather than
; an unusual way of writing a quantity.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What a verified destruction receipt does not establish, stated here because a reimplementer is most likely
; to over-read exactly this, and because the sentence is the artifact's honest half.
;
; It is not a claim that the material is unreachable anywhere. It states a reach per copy class the format
; enumerates, which is a claim about the copies a deployment holds and about nothing else. A reviewer who
; wants completeness has to ask which copy classes exist for a given deployment, and this document answers
; that question by naming its own list and refusing to leave a member of it unstated, not by asserting that
; the list is everything.
;
; It is not a proof that the destroyed key is unusable. It proves that a key other than the one it names
; sealed the statement. Those are different findings and the second one is all the bytes carry.
;
; It is not a digest of what was destroyed in the sense of a content hash a reviewer can compare against
; plaintext it holds. `object` hashes the whole signed document the destruction took, exactly as it was
; handed to the deployment, so a reviewer holding that file compares a file hash and learns nothing about
; what was inside it. Reading the bytes a designation names is the reviewer's own choice and this format
; requires no reader to make it.
;
; It is not a verdict about a duty, a lawful basis, a request or a period. Nothing in this layout states that
; an erasure was owed, that it was owed to anyone, or that completing it satisfied anything. A reader holding
; only these bytes can say that the holder of the signing key stated, at one instant, a reach over five copy
; classes and the destruction of one object and one key. Whether that statement settles anything is settled
; outside this format by whoever holds the mapping and the law.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
